/// The type a column declares, as the model holds it: its DataType (a system type, [nvarchar], or an alias, [dbo].[ShortCode]) and five facets. Equality only; no type is wider or narrower than another (decision 2.9).
public sealed record ColumnType(Name Type, int Length, bool IsMax, int Precision, int Scale, string? Collation)
{
    public bool IsAlias => Type.Schema is not null;
    /// The declared type of a Column element: the single DataType target's Name and the column's Length, IsMax, Precision, Scale, Collation. A column with no DataType target is the error model.unresolved.
    public static Result<ColumnType> Of(Element column);
    /// The type SQL Server stores: this type for a system type; for an alias, the alias element's Type target with its UddtLength, UddtIsMax, UddtPrecision and UddtScale, and this column's Collation. An alias the model does not hold is model.unresolved.
    public Result<ColumnType> Stored(IReadOnlyDictionary<ElementKey, Element> model);
}

Read Value.Integer as int (checked; overflow is model.unresolved's sibling: keep it simple with checked conversion that cannot overflow for DacFx facets). Do not implement IComparable: Column orders by ordinal, so no SortedArray holds a ColumnType directly. WP 2.1 later calls ColumnType.Of on the before and after element of an altered column for Fits; WP 2.3 renders Stored as TRY_CONVERT's target type through ScriptDom.

