Algorithm, deterministic: V is schema.Tables in Name order. For each table t and each r in t.References there is an edge t → r.Parent (child to parent, self-edges included), hard when !r.Optional and soft when r.Optional. Adjacency is sorted by (parent Name, reference Key). (1) Tarjan SCC over all edges, iterative, visiting V in order. A component is cyclic when it has more than one table or a self-edge. (2) In each cyclic component, every soft edge with both ends inside becomes a Deferral. This is simple and visible rather than minimal: no feedback-set solver, the line v3 draws against v2's 977-line pass. Run Tarjan again on the component's hard edges only. Each cyclic hard SCC is a Cycle; its Path is the shortest hard cycle through its smallest-named table, found by BFS with ties broken by Name, so a hard self-edge gives the path [t]. (3) Order: build the condensation DAG of all non-deferred edges (hard-cycle SCCs as blocks). Run Kahn's algorithm with a min-heap on each block's smallest table Name: a block is ready when all its parent blocks are placed, so parents come first. Inside a hard-cycle block the members go in Name order (v2's PartialTopological). (4) Positions 0..n-1. Cost is O((V+E) log V). Expected on the golden copy with the fixture change: Account before Customer before CustomerAddress; Status before Order before OrderLine before OrderLineNote; Category before Product; Department before Employee, with Deferred = {FK_Department_Head, the unnamed Employee.ManagerId self-reference}; Cycles = {[dbo].[Egg] → [dbo].[Hen]}.
